## P2: an older workflow's cancelled re-run still strands the exact revert

At warrant/gate.py:1521-1526, approve_held chooses each workflow's highest workflow run id. check_reasons (lines 589-610) instead chooses the highest check run id. Those orders need not agree across workflow re-runs. A re-run keeps its workflow run ID and the original SHA; its attempt and check are later. The retry therefore still differs from the merge predicate, despite T's assertion that #562 made them agree.

Concrete sequence on the same exact revert head:

1. Workflow run 5 exists; workflow run 9 subsequently completes successfully, producing required check 10.
2. Run 5 is re-run later (attempt 2) and cancelled, producing required check 20 in suite 5. Run 9 remains successful.
3. The actual gate predicate reads check 20, returns CHECK_RED, and recognizes facts.revert as the braked merge's exact revert.
4. approve_held reads run 9 as latest, counts its success as live, and dispatches nothing. Two invocations both dispatch nothing. enforce_brakes skips a new revert while this exact one is pending, so the owner's veto remains unenforced without an outside action.

Expected: align the retry with the required check/attempt the merge predicate actually selected, and retry its cancelled workflow. Do not treat a workflow's original numeric ID as the chronology of its attempts. Preserve refusal of genuinely failed checks and avoid retrying an unrelated live attempt.

GitHub's primary documentation confirms the underlying rerun identity: GITHUB_RUN_ID does not change on re-run; GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT increments. Re-runs use the original GITHUB_SHA and GITHUB_REF.
- https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/variables
- https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/manage-workflow-runs/re-run-workflows-and-jobs

